NNAAMMEE
       env - run a program in a modified environment

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       eennvv [_O_P_T_I_O_N]... [_-] [_N_A_M_E_=_V_A_L_U_E]... [_C_O_M_M_A_N_D [_A_R_G]...]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       Set each NAME to VALUE in the environment and run COMMAND.

       Mandatory  arguments  to  long  options are mandatory for short options
       too.

       --ii, ----iiggnnoorree--eennvviirroonnmmeenntt
              start with an empty environment

       --00, ----nnuullll
              end each output line with NUL, not newline

       --uu, ----uunnsseett=_N_A_M_E
              remove variable from the environment

       --CC, ----cchhddiirr=_D_I_R
              change working directory to DIR

       --SS, ----sspplliitt--ssttrriinngg=_S
              process and split S into separate arguments; used to pass multi‐
              ple arguments on shebang lines

       ----bblloocckk--ssiiggnnaall[=_S_I_G]
              block delivery of SIG signal(s) to COMMAND

       ----ddeeffaauulltt--ssiiggnnaall[=_S_I_G]
              reset handling of SIG signal(s) to the default

       ----iiggnnoorree--ssiiggnnaall[=_S_I_G]
              set handling of SIG signal(s) to do nothing

       ----lliisstt--ssiiggnnaall--hhaannddlliinngg
              list non default signal handling to stderr

       --vv, ----ddeebbuugg
              print verbose information for each processing step

       ----hheellpp display this help and exit

       ----vveerrssiioonn
              output version information and exit

       A mere - implies --ii.  If no COMMAND, print the resulting environment.

       SIG  may  be  a  signal name like 'PIPE', or a signal number like '13'.
       Without SIG, all known signals are included.  Multiple signals  can  be
       comma-separated.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
   --SS//----sspplliitt--ssttrriinngg uussaaggee iinn ssccrriippttss
       The  --SS option allows specifying multiple parameters in a script.  Run‐
       ning a script named 11..ppll containing the following first line:

              #!/usr/bin/env -S perl -w -T
              ...

       Will execute ppeerrll --ww --TT 11..ppll ..

       Without the ''--SS'' parameter the script will likely fail with:

              /usr/bin/env: 'perl -w -T': No such file or directory

       See the full documentation for more details.

   ----ddeeffaauulltt--ssiiggnnaall[[==SSIIGG]] uussaaggee
       This option allows setting a signal  handler  to  its  default  action,
       which  is  not  possible using the traditional shell trap command.  The
       following example ensures that seq will be  terminated  by  SIGPIPE  no
       matter  how  this  signal  is being handled in the process invoking the
       command.


              sh -c 'env --default-signal=PIPE seq inf | head -n1'

NNOOTTEESS
       POSIX's eexxeecc(3p) pages says:
              "many existing applications wrongly assume that they start  with
              certain  signals  set to the default action and/or unblocked....
              Therefore, it is best not to block or ignore signals across  ex‐
              ecs  without  explicit  reason  to  do so, and especially not to
              block signals across execs of arbitrary (not  closely  cooperat‐
              ing) programs."

